KPL/IK


Artemis 2 Handheld Cameras Instrument Kernel
===============================================================================

   This Instrument Kernel (IK) file contains Field-Of-View (FOV) definitions 
   and other parameters for various Artemis 2 Orion handheld cameras -- 
   Nikon D5 cameras (NKD5015, NKD5017), Nikon Z9 camera (NKZ9019) and 
   iPhone 17 Pro Max main and telephoto cameras (IPHONE).


Version and Date
----------------------------------------------------------

   Version 0.0 -- October 1, 2026 -- Boris Semenov, NAIF/JPL

      Initial version.


References
----------------------------------------------------------

   1. Kernel Pool Required Reading

   2. Frames Required Reading

   3. Artemis 2 Frames Kernel, latest version

   4. Artemis 2 OSDMP, various versions

   5. A2_Camera_Sensor_Specs spreadsheet, by David Hollibaugh Baker


Contact Information
----------------------------------------------------------

   Boris Semenov, NAIF/JPL, (818)-354-8136, Boris.Semenov@jpl.nasa.gov


Implementation Notes
----------------------------------------------------------

   This file is used by the SPICE system as follows: programs that make
   use of this frame kernel must `load' the kernel, normally during
   program initialization. Loading the kernel associates the data items
   with their names in a data structure called the "kernel pool". The
   SPICE API FURNSH loads a kernel into the pool as shown below:

      FORTRAN (SPICELIB):       CALL FURNSH ( instrument_kernel_name )

      C (CSPICE):               furnsh_c ( instrument_kernel_name );

      IDL (ICY):                cspice_furnsh, instrument_kernel_name

      MATLAB (MICE):            cspice_furnsh ( 'instrument_kernel_name' )

      PYTHON (SPICEYPY):        furnsh( instrument_kernel_name )

   This file was created and may be updated with a text editor or word
   processor.


Naming Conventions
----------------------------------------------------------

   All names referencing values in this I-kernel start with the
   characters `INS' followed by the NAIF Artemis 2 spacecraft ID number
   (-24) followed by a NAIF three digit ID code for one of the
   handheld cameras, defined in [3] as:


      ART002_NKD5015                -24310
      ART002_NKD5015_FL14           -24311
      ART002_NKD5015_FL24           -24312
      ART002_NKD5015_FL35           -24313
      ART002_NKD5015_FL80           -24314
      ART002_NKD5015_FL400          -24315

      ART002_NKD5017                -24320
      ART002_NKD5017_FL14           -24321
      ART002_NKD5017_FL24           -24322
      ART002_NKD5017_FL35           -24323
      ART002_NKD5017_FL80           -24324
      ART002_NKD5017_FL400          -24325

      ART002_NKZ9019                -24330
      ART002_NKZ9019_FL14           -24331
      ART002_NKZ9019_FL24           -24332
      ART002_NKZ9019_FL35           -24333
      ART002_NKZ9019_FL80           -24334
      ART002_NKZ9019_FL400          -24335

      ART002_IPHONE                 -24340
      ART002_IPHONE_TELE            -24341

   (NKD5015 = Nikon D5 with SN ending in 015, NKD5017 NKD5015 = Nikon
   D5 with SN ending in 017, NKZ9019 = Nikon Z9 with SN ending in 019,
   IPHONE =  iPhone 17 Pro Max, _FLnn = focal length "nn" optics, 
   _TELE = iPhone telephoto camera).

   Nikon cameras without the _FLnn suffix provide parameters for the
   35 mm optics.
 
   The remainder of the name is an underscore character followed by the
   unique name of the data item. For example, the NKD5015 camera boresight
   direction in the NKD5015 camera frame (``ART002_NKD5015'' -- see [3])
   is specified by:

      INS-24310_BORESIGHT

   The upper bound on the length of the name of any data item is 32
   characters.
 
   If the same item is included in more than one file, or if the same
   item appears more than once within a single file, the latest value
   supersedes any earlier values.


Handheld Camera Descriptions
---------------------------------------------------------

   The Artemis 2 handheld cameras are described in [4].


Mounting Alignment
--------------------------------------------------------

   Refer to the latest version of the Artemis 2 Frames Definition Kernel
   (FK) [3] for the Artemis 2 handheld camera reference frame definitions and
   mounting alignment information.


Apparent FOV Layout
--------------------------------------------------------

   This section provides a diagram illustrating the handheld cameras'
   apparent FOV layout in their corresponding reference frames.

   Pixel (1,1) is in the top left corner of the image, and pixel
   ("Pixels","Lines") is in the bottom right corner. +X, +Y, +Z axes
   are defined in the Artemis 2 Frames Definition Kernel (FK) [3].


                                     Boresight (+Z axis)
                                        is into the page

            (0,0)    "Pixels" pixels/line
            pixel *-----------------------+            ---
                  |                       |             ^
                  |                       |             |
                  |                       |             |
                  |        +Z             |  +X         |
          "Lines" |           x------------->           | "YSIZE"
           lines  |           |           |             |  deg
                  |           |           |             |
                  |           |           |             |
                  |           |           |             v
                  +-----------|-----------*            ---
                              |  ("Pixels","Lines")
                              v         pixel
                           +Y


                  |      "XSIZE" deg      |
                  |<--------------------->|
                  |                       |


   The "XSIZE", "YSIZE", "Pixels", and "Lines" parameters used in the
   diagram are shown in the summary tables and are incorporated into
   the corresponding IK keywords in the sections below.


Handheld Camera FOVs
----------------------------------------------------------

   This table summarizes the handheld camera FOV parameters:

   Name                 ID     Shape XSIZE  YSIZE     Frame     BSIGHT Cross
   -------------------- ------ ---- ------ ------ -------------- ----- -----
   ART002_NKD5015       -24310 RECT 27.102 18.839 ART002_NKD5015 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5015_FL14  -24311 RECT 51.989 40.463 ART002_NKD5015 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5015_FL24  -24312 RECT 36.736 26.453 ART002_NKD5015 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5015_FL35  -24313 RECT 27.102 18.839 ART002_NKD5015 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5015_FL80  -24314 RECT 12.621  8.490 ART002_NKD5015 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5015_FL400 -24315 RECT  2.564  1.710 ART002_NKD5015 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5017       -24320 RECT 27.102 18.839 ART002_NKD5017 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5017_FL14  -24321 RECT 51.989 40.463 ART002_NKD5017 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5017_FL24  -24322 RECT 36.736 26.453 ART002_NKD5017 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5017_FL35  -24323 RECT 27.102 18.839 ART002_NKD5017 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5017_FL80  -24324 RECT 12.621  8.490 ART002_NKD5017 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKD5017_FL400 -24325 RECT  2.564  1.710 ART002_NKD5017 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKZ9019       -24330 RECT 27.133 18.862 ART002_NKZ9019 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKZ9019_FL14  -24331 RECT 52.026 40.501 ART002_NKZ9019 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKZ9019_FL24  -24332 RECT 36.772 26.484 ART002_NKZ9019 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKZ9019_FL35  -24333 RECT 27.133 18.862 ART002_NKZ9019 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKZ9019_FL80  -24334 RECT 12.637  8.501 ART002_NKZ9019 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_NKZ9019_FL400 -24335 RECT  2.567  1.712 ART002_NKZ9019 0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_IPHONE        -24340 RECT 35.270 27.943 ART002_IPHONE  0,0,1 1,0,0
   ART002_IPHONE_TELE   -24341 RECT  9.866  7.431 ART002_IPHONE  0,0,1 1,0,0

   The XSIZE and YSIZE are half-extents in degrees computed using the 
   detector and optical parameters from the next section as follows:

      XSIZE = atan( Pixels * Pix size / 2, FL ) 

      YSIZE = atan( Lines  * Pix size / 2, FL )

   The keywords below define FOVs based on the parameters above.

